// Places for people, to put a road between.
//
//     import 'buildings' { House, Row, Tower }
//
//     object House(4)
//     object Row(5, 'winter')      { translate X 12 }
//     object Tower(14, 'summer', 3) { translate X -14 }
//
// | `House` | One dwelling: plinth, walls, a gabled roof, a door and windows. |
// | `Row` | Several houses joined shoulder to shoulder, as a terrace. |
// | `Tower` | A taller block, bay after bay, with a parapet rather than a roof. |
//
// The three numbers mean what they mean in the other libraries -- **how big**, **what time of year**,
// and **which one of that kind** -- and only the first is required.  Height is the eaves-to-ground
// measure for a house and the whole height for a tower; for a `Row` the first number is **how many
// houses**, since a terrace is counted rather than measured.
//
// **The season puts snow on the roofs** and does nothing else, which is the whole of what winter does
// to a building from the outside.  A building is not deciduous; it takes the word so that a scene
// setting its season once gets roofs that agree with the ground.
//
// -- What makes a box into a building --------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This library exists because of a scene that failed at it.  `a-block-of-buildings` in the gallery
// raises eleven towers out of one loop, and says of itself that they are *boxes with lights on, not
// architecture* -- a real building has a plan, a frontage, and a way of meeting the ground.  Four
// things do most of that work, and all four are cheap:
//
// **A plinth.**  A wall that meets the ground in a line reads as a sheet standing on grass.  A course
// at the bottom, a little proud of the wall and a little lighter, is how a building sits down.
//
// **Depth in the window.**  A rectangle of dark paint on a wall is a picture of a window.  What reads
// is the *reveal*: the glass set back, a frame standing proud around it, and a sill that overhangs and
// throws a small shadow.  The first try here laid bars *across* the opening instead of bordering it,
// and every window came out as a white plus sign stuck to the wall.
//
// **An overhang.**  Eaves that reach past the wall lay a shadow line along the top of the facade.
// Without it a roof looks glued on; with it the roof is clearly above and the wall clearly below.
//
// **A top that is not a cut.**  A gable, or a parapet standing above the roof line.  A box ended flat
// at the top is the single strongest tell that a thing is a box.
//
// The gable is worth a word.  Its pitch is forty-five degrees, and that is not a style choice: it lets
// the end wall be cut as the top half of a square stood on its corner, which is one `intersection` of
// two boxes.  A cube turned a quarter is a *diamond*, not a triangle -- turning one and hoping leaves
// a diamond sticking up through the ridge, which is what the first attempt did.
//
// -- What is here for the library and not for you --------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything beginning with `Build` is the machinery.  The materials arrive in your scene whether you
// asked or not, for the reason the other libraries give -- a material is looked up where it is *used*
// -- so they carry the prefix.

// -- What buildings are made of --------------------------------------------------------------------

BuildWall = material { pigment [0.74, 0.70, 0.62]  specular 0.05  shininess 16 }
BuildWallWarm = material { pigment [0.66, 0.53, 0.44]  specular 0.05  shininess 16 }
BuildWallCool = material { pigment [0.62, 0.63, 0.61]  specular 0.06  shininess 18 }
BuildTrim = material { pigment [0.90, 0.88, 0.84]  specular 0.12  shininess 30 }
BuildRoof = material { pigment [0.30, 0.26, 0.25]  specular 0.10  shininess 20 }
BuildRoofRed = material { pigment [0.42, 0.24, 0.18]  specular 0.10  shininess 20 }
BuildGlass = material { pigment [0.09, 0.12, 0.15]  specular 0.55  shininess 140  reflective 0.22 }
BuildDoor = material { pigment [0.28, 0.18, 0.14]  specular 0.20  shininess 40 }
BuildSnow = material { pigment [0.93, 0.94, 0.97]  specular 0.25  shininess 50 }

// Three walls and two roofs, so a row does not come out as one house repeated.  Which one a building
// wears is its variant's business.
primitive BuildWallOf(variant) -> material {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return material BuildWall }
        case 1 { return material BuildWallWarm }
        default { return material BuildWallCool }
    }
}

primitive BuildRoofOf(variant) -> material {
    if (mod(floor(variant), 2) == 0) {
        return material BuildRoof
    }
    else {
        return material BuildRoofRed
    }
}

// -- A window ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// An opening set back into the wall, a frame standing proud *around* it, and a sill under it that
// reaches further out than anything else and throws the small shadow that sells the whole thing.
//
// It is placed with its middle on the face of the wall, so the glass falls behind and the frame in
// front.
primitive BuildWindow(wide, tall, deep) -> group {
    return group {
        cube { material BuildGlass  scale [wide, tall, deep]  translate Z -deep * 1.4 }

        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.18, tall * 0.09, deep]  translate Y tall * 1.09 }
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.30, tall * 0.10, deep * 2.2]
               translate Y -tall * 1.10 }
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 0.10, tall, deep]  translate X -wide * 1.10 }
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 0.10, tall, deep]  translate X wide * 1.10 }
    }
}

// -- A house -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The snow that lies on a pitched roof, which is nothing at all for three seasons of the four.  It is
// two slabs laid on the two slopes, a shade proud of them, so what shows is a white edge along the
// ridge and the eaves rather than a white roof.
primitive BuildRoofSnow(season, wide, deep, height) -> group {
    switch (season) {
        case 'winter' {
            // Laid a little way out along each slope's own normal.  Set at the slope's own offset
            // it disappears *inside* the roof, which is what the first attempt did -- snow has to be
            // pushed out, not merely placed.
            return group {
                cube { material BuildSnow  scale [wide * 0.84, height * 0.016, deep * 1.19]
                       rotate Z 45
                       translate [-wide * 0.56 - height * 0.026, wide * 0.56 + height * 0.026, 0] }
                cube { material BuildSnow  scale [wide * 0.84, height * 0.016, deep * 1.19]
                       rotate Z -45
                       translate [wide * 0.56 + height * 0.026, wide * 0.56 + height * 0.026, 0] }
            }
        }
        default { return group { } }
    }
}

// Snow on a flat top, which is what a parapet leaves.  It lies *across* the parapet rather than down
// inside it, and that is the whole point: snow lying inside a parapet is invisible from any camera
// standing on the ground, so a tower's season would do nothing at all in the scenes towers are
// actually in.  A cap on the wall shows as the bright line along the top of a snowy block, which is
// what you see from the street.
primitive BuildFlatSnow(season, wide, deep) -> group {
    switch (season) {
        case 'winter' {
            return group {
                cube { material BuildSnow  scale [wide, wide * 0.025, deep] }
            }
        }
        default { return group { } }
    }
}

// The shell of a house without its windows, so that a row can build one of these per dwelling and
// hang whatever windows that dwelling wants on it.
primitive BuildHouseShell(height, season, variant, wide, deep) -> group {
    walls = height * 0.50

    return group {
        // How it meets the ground.
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.04, height * 0.03, deep * 1.04]
               translate Y height * 0.03 }
        cube { material BuildWallOf(variant)  scale [wide, walls, deep]
               translate Y walls + height * 0.06 }

        group {
            // The end walls, cut as the top half of a diamond -- see the note at the head of this file.
            intersection {
                cube { rotate Z 45  scale wide * 0.7071 }
                cube { scale [wide * 1.2, wide, deep]  translate Y wide }
                material BuildWallOf(variant)
                translate Z deep * 0.99
            }
            intersection {
                cube { rotate Z 45  scale wide * 0.7071 }
                cube { scale [wide * 1.2, wide, deep]  translate Y wide }
                material BuildWallOf(variant)
                translate Z -deep * 0.99
            }

            // The slopes, reaching past the wall so the eaves overhang and lay their shadow.
            cube { material BuildRoofOf(variant)  scale [wide * 0.80, height * 0.020, deep * 1.14]
                   rotate Z 45  translate [-wide * 0.56, wide * 0.56, 0] }
            cube { material BuildRoofOf(variant)  scale [wide * 0.80, height * 0.020, deep * 1.14]
                   rotate Z -45  translate [wide * 0.56, wide * 0.56, 0] }

            object BuildRoofSnow(season, wide, deep, height)

            translate Y walls * 2 + height * 0.06
        }

        cube { material BuildRoofOf(variant)  scale [wide * 0.10, height * 0.085, wide * 0.10]
               translate [wide * 0.42, walls * 2 + height * 0.30, -deep * 0.35] }
    }
}

primitive House(height, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    wide = height * 0.34
    deep = height * 0.30
    walls = height * 0.50

    return group {
        object BuildHouseShell(height, season, variant, wide, deep)

        for side in [0, 1] {
            across = (side - 0.5) * wide * 0.90

            object BuildWindow(wide * 0.13, walls * 0.20, deep * 0.06) {
                translate [across, walls * 0.55, -deep]
            }
            object BuildWindow(wide * 0.13, walls * 0.20, deep * 0.06) {
                translate [across, walls * 1.45, -deep]
            }
        }

        cube { material BuildDoor  scale [wide * 0.11, walls * 0.30, deep * 0.03]
               translate [0, walls * 0.36, -deep] }

        // A house set exactly square to the road is a house nobody built.
        rotate Y (random(variant, 61) - 0.5) * 7
    }
}

// -- A row -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Houses shoulder to shoulder.  They share a height and a depth, because a terrace was built at once,
// and differ in their walls, their roofs and their windows, because the people in them did not.
primitive Row(houses, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    height = 3.6
    wide = height * 0.34
    deep = height * 0.30
    walls = height * 0.50

    return group {
        for house in [0, houses - 1] {
            group {
                object BuildHouseShell(height, season, variant * 13 + house, wide, deep)

                for side in [0, 1] {
                    across = (side - 0.5) * wide * 0.90

                    object BuildWindow(wide * 0.13, walls * 0.20, deep * 0.06) {
                        translate [across, walls * 0.55, -deep]
                    }
                    object BuildWindow(wide * 0.13, walls * 0.20, deep * 0.06) {
                        translate [across, walls * 1.45, -deep]
                    }
                }

                cube { material BuildDoor  scale [wide * 0.11, walls * 0.30, deep * 0.03]
                       translate [0, walls * 0.36, -deep] }

                translate X house * wide * 2 - (houses - 1) * wide
            }
        }
    }
}

// -- A tower -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Taller, and ended with a parapet rather than a roof: a low wall standing above the top floor, which
// is what stops a tall box from looking cut off.  The bays are regular because a building's windows
// are regular -- what varies between towers is how many bays and how tall a floor is, not where any
// one window landed.
primitive Tower(height, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    wide = height * (0.15 + random(variant, 71) * 0.06)
    deep = wide * (0.72 + random(variant, 72) * 0.4)
    // A storey is a storey whatever the building is: a taller tower has *more* floors, not taller
    // ones.  Deriving the count from the height as a fraction gave every tower the same eight.
    floors = max(3, floor(height / 2.2))
    storey = height / floors
    bays = 2 + floor(random(variant, 73) * 3)

    return group {
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.05, height * 0.018, deep * 1.05]
               translate Y height * 0.018 }
        cube { material BuildWallOf(variant)  scale [wide, height * 0.5, deep]
               translate Y height * 0.5 + height * 0.03 }

        // The parapet: a band standing above the top floor, and a course under it.
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.04, height * 0.012, deep * 1.04]
               translate Y height * 1.03 }
        cube { material BuildWallOf(variant)  scale [wide * 1.02, height * 0.030, deep * 1.02]
               translate Y height * 1.06 }

        for level in [1, floors - 1] {
            for bay in [0, bays - 1] {
                across = (bay - (bays - 1) * 0.5) * wide * 1.6 / bays

                object BuildWindow(wide * 0.5 / bays, storey * 0.26, deep * 0.05) {
                    translate [across, level * storey + storey * 0.45, -deep]
                }
            }
        }

        object BuildFlatSnow(season, wide * 1.06, deep * 1.06) { translate Y height * 1.09 }

        rotate Y (random(variant, 74) - 0.5) * 6
    }
}
